WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.659
A customer comes to the corner shop's counter.

2
00:00:05.659 --> 00:00:08.522
The teapot from her gift box arrived cracked.

3
00:00:08.522 --> 00:00:09.921
So who sorts that out?

4
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:12.621
The shop has a simple habit.

5
00:00:12.621 --> 00:00:16.755
The shop assistant can sort out anything worth up to 5.00 herself.

6
00:00:16.755 --> 00:00:20.889
Anything bigger goes to the manager, who can sort out up to 50.00.

7
00:00:20.889 --> 00:00:24.334
The rest goes to head office, which answers everything.

8
00:00:24.334 --> 00:00:25.023
Eventually.

9
00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:28.898
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

10
00:00:28.898 --> 00:00:31.802
Why not write one method that knows all of this?

11
00:00:31.802 --> 00:00:36.401
An if for the assistant, an if for the manager, and an else for head office.

12
00:00:36.401 --> 00:00:38.035
Why build anything fancier?

13
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:41.041
Let's try it, and watch it grow.

14
00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:42.423
First the two limits.

15
00:00:42.423 --> 00:00:44.136
Then: no receipt, no help.

16
00:00:44.136 --> 00:00:46.111
Then: one complaint per order.

17
00:00:46.111 --> 00:00:49.865
Then the complaints book, which writes down every answer.

18
00:00:49.865 --> 00:00:52.499
Every new rule lands in the same method.

19
00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:54.730
It's not really a method any more.

20
00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.523
It's the whole staff, squeezed onto one chair.

21
00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:04.500
What we'd like is for each person to know only two things: their own job, and that somebody comes after them.

22
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.315
And that's exactly how the real counter works.

23
00:01:08.315 --> 00:01:11.611
Nobody needs to know who sits further down the line.

24
00:01:11.611 --> 00:01:15.603
Each person looks at the complaint, and does one of two things.

25
00:01:15.603 --> 00:01:16.237
Answer it.

26
00:01:16.237 --> 00:01:17.124
Or pass it on.

27
00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:18.725
Let's map it.

28
00:01:18.725 --> 00:01:20.641
The complaint is the request.

29
00:01:20.641 --> 00:01:23.084
Each person in the line is a handler.

30
00:01:23.084 --> 00:01:27.774
Passing it on is a call to next: the rest of the line, whoever that is.

31
00:01:27.774 --> 00:01:29.953
And head office is the last stop.

32
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:35.502
In code, a handler is a small function of the complaint and next.

33
00:01:35.502 --> 00:01:36.999
Here's the assistant.

34
00:01:36.999 --> 00:01:39.923
The shop counts in cents, so 5.00 is 500.

35
00:01:39.923 --> 00:01:41.278
Worth that or less?

36
00:01:41.278 --> 00:01:43.417
Answer now: assistant, sorted.

37
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:45.985
And the rest of the line never runs.

38
00:01:45.985 --> 00:01:47.553
Otherwise, pass it on.

39
00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:50.354
Now the whole desk, as a list.

40
00:01:50.354 --> 00:01:51.990
First the complaints book.

41
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:55.011
Then the receipt check: no receipt, turned away.

42
00:01:55.011 --> 00:01:59.667
Then once per order: a second complaint about the same order, turned away.

43
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:02.751
Then the assistant, the manager, and head office.

44
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:05.269
The book is the interesting one.

45
00:02:05.269 --> 00:02:08.112
It calls next first, and writes its line after.

46
00:02:08.112 --> 00:02:10.955
So on the way in, the book does nothing at all.

47
00:02:10.955 --> 00:02:14.645
It waits for the reply to come back, and then writes it down.

48
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.568
That's the shape of every handler.

49
00:02:17.568 --> 00:02:19.864
Code before next runs on the way in.

50
00:02:19.864 --> 00:02:23.244
Code after next runs on the way back, with the reply.

51
00:02:23.244 --> 00:02:25.731
And no next at all ends the trip early.

52
00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:28.978
Now four customers come in.

53
00:02:28.978 --> 00:02:32.599
One: the cracked teapot, worth 18.00, with a receipt.

54
00:02:32.599 --> 00:02:34.444
Two: the same teapot again.

55
00:02:34.444 --> 00:02:38.475
Three: stale bread, worth 2.50, but the receipt is at home.

56
00:02:38.475 --> 00:02:41.481
Four: the same bread, back with the receipt.

57
00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:43.116
Who answers each one?

58
00:02:43.116 --> 00:02:45.532
And how many complaints get sorted?

59
00:02:45.532 --> 00:02:46.636
Pause and guess.

60
00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:53.369
Here's what the lesson's ComplaintDeskDemo printed.

61
00:02:53.369 --> 00:02:58.173
The teapot is worth 18.00, too much for the assistant, so the manager sorts it.

62
00:02:58.333 --> 00:03:02.756
The second time, once per order stops it: we sorted this one already.

63
00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:06.040
The bread without a receipt: please bring the receipt.

64
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:09.261
And when she comes back with it, the assistant sorts it.

65
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:10.845
Two complaints sorted.

66
00:03:10.845 --> 00:03:12.224
And look at the book.

67
00:03:12.224 --> 00:03:18.593
It wrote a line for all four, the turned away ones too, because every reply travels back past it.

68
00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:22.567
That's where the shop picture breaks, in a useful way.

69
00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:27.456
In the shop, an assistant who passes a complaint on may never hear how it ended.

70
00:03:27.456 --> 00:03:35.095
In code, the reply comes back through every handler that passed it on, and each one can read it, write it down, or change it.

71
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.866
Now let's break it on purpose.

72
00:03:37.866 --> 00:03:42.551
Swap two handlers, so once per order comes before the receipt check.

73
00:03:42.551 --> 00:03:44.204
The same four customers.

74
00:03:44.204 --> 00:03:45.099
What changes?

75
00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:46.201
Pause and guess.

76
00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.932
The bread without a receipt is marked as handled first, and only then turned away.

77
00:03:54.932 --> 00:03:59.876
So when she comes back with the receipt, she hears: we sorted this one already.

78
00:03:59.876 --> 00:04:01.191
Complaints sorted: 1.

79
00:04:01.333 --> 00:04:01.699
Well.

80
00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:06.083
She now has a receipt, a complaint, and the same stale loaf.

81
00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:09.932
So the point is this: the order of the list is behavior.

82
00:04:09.932 --> 00:04:13.204
Moving two lines changed what a customer was told.

83
00:04:13.204 --> 00:04:14.905
Pin the order with a test.

84
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.997
What we built has a name: Chain of Responsibility.

85
00:04:18.997 --> 00:04:25.712
A request travels along a list of handlers, and each handler either answers it, or passes it to the next.

86
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.007
The 1994 book of design patterns links handlers with a setNext method.

87
00:04:31.007 --> 00:04:32.125
We don't need it.

88
00:04:32.125 --> 00:04:37.785
Start from the last stop, and wrap it in each handler, from the last one to the first.

89
00:04:37.785 --> 00:04:44.432
That walk is called a fold, and the chain it builds can't change, so one desk serves every complaint.

90
00:04:45.333 --> 00:04:46.339
Two guardrails.

91
00:04:46.339 --> 00:04:50.965
Every path through a handler must answer, or call next, exactly once.

92
00:04:50.965 --> 00:04:56.664
Forget next, and in Java it's tempting to return null: the complaint just disappears.

93
00:04:56.664 --> 00:05:00.151
Call next twice, and everything after it runs twice.

94
00:05:00.151 --> 00:05:02.028
Two new teapots in the post.

95
00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:09.417
And work for the way back, like the book's line, belongs in a finally block, so an exception further down can't skip it.

96
00:05:10.333 --> 00:05:12.162
So, why not one big method?

97
00:05:12.162 --> 00:05:16.634
Because in a chain, each person knows one job, and one word: next.

98
00:05:16.634 --> 00:05:18.598
The list decides who answers.

99
00:05:18.598 --> 00:05:22.392
And the reply comes back past everyone who passed it on.

100
00:05:22.533 --> 00:05:23.463
Quick check.

101
00:05:23.463 --> 00:05:29.432
A customer brings a complaint worth 60.00, with a receipt, about a new order.

102
00:05:29.432 --> 00:05:30.362
Who answers?

103
00:05:30.362 --> 00:05:32.843
And does the book write it down?

104
00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:40.641
Head office, because 60.00 is more than the manager can sort.

105
00:05:40.641 --> 00:05:41.241
And yes.

106
00:05:41.241 --> 00:05:45.815
The reply travels back through the book, so it gets its line.
